# Living rulebook (every session)

[`docs/RULEBOOK.md`](docs/RULEBOOK.md) is how the game **currently plays**.
The source for that text is [`docs/GAME_STATE.md`](docs/GAME_STATE.md).
[`docs/OPEN_DESIGN.md`](docs/OPEN_DESIGN.md) is the unsettled questions.
Print vocabulary is the whole of [`docs/KEYWORDS.md`](docs/KEYWORDS.md). The Rules tab
shows both.

## Must update in the same change

If you change `src/server` (or `GameRulesConfig` defaults) so a player would
notice different timing, legality, costs, phases, zones, victory, or loadout
constraints, **edit `docs/RULEBOOK.md` in that same change**. Specs and
`OPEN_DESIGN.md` do not replace this.

Examples that **require** a rulebook edit: a new or changed phase, Meter gain
or spend, the sequence, Tag, Assist, the Chain, damage order, draw rate,
deck size, or the win condition.

Examples that **do not**: a new card using existing effects (still update
`docs/KEYWORDS.md` if you add or rename a keyword); UI/store/PeerJS except the
Rules tab rendering of the rulebook/glossary; metrics; parked print in
`DEFERRED_CATALOGUE.md` with no engine behavior.

If you add a token, operator, or physics keyword, edit `docs/KEYWORDS.md` in
that same change (`.cursor/rules/keywords.mdc`).

If the question is still open in `docs/OPEN_DESIGN.md`, do not write it as fact.
Point at that file. After a rule is settled, the rulebook is the player-facing
wording. The source file is updated in the same change.

Engine work: this is part of DoD for `engine-developer` / `develop-engine` /
`implement-hooks`. Catalogue-only work hands the rulebook to engine-developer
when a new mechanic ships.
